PWM CONTROL AND VERIFICATION

Current pwmctl.c syntax:
  pwmctl status
  pwmctl freq HZ      frequency 8..100000 Hz
  pwmctl a DUTY       GP18 channel A duty 0..100 percent
  pwmctl b DUTY       GP19 channel B duty 0..100 percent
  pwmctl off          both channel duties to 0

On this teaching setup GP18 is connected to a BUZZER and GP19
is connected to a SERVO MOTOR. Both channels use PWM slice 1 and
SHARE the frequency. Duty has 0.1 percent steps. Changing the
frequency affects BOTH devices; it is not safe to treat them as
independent PWM outputs.

READ-ONLY CHECK (safe default with servo connected)
  pins
  pwmctl status

DO NOT execute a 523 Hz buzzer example with the servo attached:
frequency changes affect both outputs. Servo control needs a
servo-specific period and pulse width; duty alone is insufficient
to describe a safe position. Determine electrical/mechanical limits
and obtain approval before modifying either PWM channel.
'off' does not restore previous duty/frequency automatically.
If the test is not approved, run 'pwmctl status' ONLY.

TROUBLESHOOTING
  'value out of range': verify HZ or DUTY syntax and bounds.
  'cannot write PWM device': verify current pin ownership and
  that the expected device nodes exist.
  No audible tone: check wiring, channel, duty, active framebuffer
  or another demo; software success does not prove hardware output.
  GP19 servo: requires device-specific pulse width, period and
  safe mechanical range. GP18 buzzer cannot use an independent
  frequency while the servo shares the same PWM slice.

OBSERVATION VS CONTROL
  'pwmctl status' reads state; 'freq', 'a', 'b', 'off' change it.
  An LLM must not run a state-changing command as a discovery step.
